WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:01:10.789 --> 00:01:11.408
Versailles,

00:01:12.189 --> 00:01:15.171
the enchanted palace where all is splendor and delight,

00:01:16.112 --> 00:01:19.415
where the marriage of art and nature produce perfection.

00:01:23.278 --> 00:01:24.399
Versailles,

00:01:24.419 --> 00:01:25.179
its other face,

00:01:25.881 --> 00:01:29.103
a formidable instrument of power built by Louis XIV.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:33.320
The great Bourbon king moved the center of power away from Paris,

00:01:34.081 --> 00:01:40.362
as if to distance the court from the whispers and intrigues of the capital and the plots that have haunted him since childhood.

00:01:43.864 --> 00:01:47.064
Here the king gathers all the nobility of his realm about him,

00:01:47.965 --> 00:01:49.225
under his watchful eye.

00:01:51.306 --> 00:01:53.427
Soon more than 3,000 princes,

00:01:53.667 --> 00:01:54.187
courtiers,

00:01:54.627 --> 00:01:57.108
ministers and servants live at Versailles.

00:02:11.195 --> 00:02:11.496
Palace,

00:02:11.816 --> 00:02:14.877
park and garden are aligned with the sun's path.

00:02:15.736 --> 00:02:16.918
Here all is discipline,

00:02:17.478 --> 00:02:17.717
order,

00:02:18.298 --> 00:02:18.817
balance.

00:02:33.581 --> 00:02:33.961
Beyond

00:02:33.962 --> 00:02:36.223
the monumental esplanade of the Place d'Armes,

00:02:36.763 --> 00:02:38.943
the first gate opens onto the forecourt.

00:02:50.259 --> 00:02:50.820
The ministers'

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:53.722
quarters throng with petitioners from all over the land,

00:02:54.183 --> 00:02:57.125
who have come to Versailles to promote their own concerns.

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:02.310
The second gate,

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:03.912
destroyed during the revolution,

00:03:04.252 --> 00:03:05.753
is the real entrance to the palace.

00:03:06.574 --> 00:03:07.515
It is locked by night,

00:03:07.675 --> 00:03:09.517
but by day it is open to all.

00:03:10.258 --> 00:03:11.819
Everyone has access to the king.

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:13.521
Understandably,

00:03:13.781 --> 00:03:15.643
this freedom astonishes foreigners.

00:03:17.979 --> 00:03:19.941
Guards stand by to confiscate firearms,

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:22.960
to search carriages authorized inside the royal courtyard,

00:03:23.561 --> 00:03:25.681
and to make sure everyone is properly dressed.

00:03:28.222 --> 00:03:30.082
But everything has been anticipated.

00:03:30.903 --> 00:03:35.464
Those who have neither the means nor the right to carry a sword and hat can rent them at the entrance.

00:03:36.745 --> 00:03:45.247
People from all walks of life rub shoulders on their spectacular marble stairway that leads to the queen's quarters on one side and to the king's on the other.

00:03:48.551 --> 00:03:50.294
After the death of Queen Marie-Thérèse,

00:03:50.933 --> 00:03:54.356
the king moves into apartments where he will live for the next 30 years.

00:03:55.038 --> 00:03:56.338
30 years lived pitilessly,

00:03:56.579 --> 00:03:58.280
exposed to the public eye.

00:04:00.462 --> 00:04:03.545
24 bodyguards live in the guardroom at all times.

00:04:04.325 --> 00:04:08.528
Four of them are assigned to protect the king from the crowd any time he moves about the palace.

00:04:11.631 --> 00:04:12.752
Here in the first anteroom,

00:04:13.212 --> 00:04:14.674
where courtiers stroll by day,

00:04:15.294 --> 00:04:16.776
the king eats the royal meal.

00:04:17.611 --> 00:04:18.192
At 10 o'clock,

00:04:18.411 --> 00:04:20.532
he displays his royal appetite to all.

00:04:24.474 --> 00:04:25.515
Before the fireplace,

00:04:25.795 --> 00:04:26.755
the royal caterers,

00:04:27.195 --> 00:04:29.076
literally attendants of the mouth,

00:04:29.697 --> 00:04:32.438
have laid the table for the sovereign and the royal family.

00:04:35.519 --> 00:04:38.681
Lords and ladies take their places in the anteroom according to rank,

00:04:39.421 --> 00:04:41.423
in the presence of curious passers-by,

00:04:41.923 --> 00:04:43.243
astounded by the spectacle.

00:04:48.108 --> 00:04:49.669
Through the windows of the second ante room,

00:04:50.209 --> 00:04:51.729
one can see the hall of mirrors.

00:04:52.749 --> 00:04:56.730
Here the courtiers in attendance wait to be admitted at the moment of the royal awakening.

00:04:57.711 --> 00:04:58.091
This room,

00:04:58.571 --> 00:04:59.712
called the bullseye,

00:05:00.391 --> 00:05:03.052
takes its name from the curious shape of its circular window,

00:05:05.013 --> 00:05:06.874
frolicking infants and cherubs.

00:05:08.174 --> 00:05:13.696
This frieze embodies the tastes of an aging king who demands that youth be ever present about him.

00:05:20.218 --> 00:05:23.621
The page boy silently opens the shutters of the royal bedchamber.

00:05:24.502 --> 00:05:26.343
Golden light suffuses the chamber.

00:05:27.084 --> 00:05:27.404
Slowly,

00:05:27.885 --> 00:05:28.766
the king awakens.

00:05:33.289 --> 00:05:36.312
The royal bed is protected by a gilded railing.

00:05:37.313 --> 00:05:37.974
Above the canopy,

00:05:38.434 --> 00:05:42.077
the personification of France watches over the sleeping king.

00:05:48.426 --> 00:05:49.547
Even more than the throne,

00:05:49.927 --> 00:05:52.108
the royal bed is the symbol of kingly power.

00:05:52.869 --> 00:05:54.870
The bedroom is at the center of the palace,

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:56.751
the bed is at the center of the bedroom,

00:05:57.171 --> 00:05:59.633
and all who pass must bow before it.

00:06:02.194 --> 00:06:02.815
At 8.30,

00:06:03.035 --> 00:06:04.216
the head chamberlain murmurs,

00:06:04.616 --> 00:06:04.976
Sire,

00:06:05.296 --> 00:06:06.197
it's time to rise.

00:06:06.997 --> 00:06:08.098
The ritual unfolds.

00:06:08.799 --> 00:06:09.039
First,

00:06:09.239 --> 00:06:10.900
the ceremony of the small awakening,

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:13.902
followed by the ceremony of the grand awakening.

00:06:19.864 --> 00:06:22.406
There is a constant stream of comings and goings.

00:06:23.087 --> 00:06:23.968
On an average morning,

00:06:24.348 --> 00:06:27.131
at least 100 people can be seen bustling in and out.

00:06:27.911 --> 00:06:29.573
Not only wardrobe and chamber attendants,

00:06:29.993 --> 00:06:30.814
but also bishops,

00:06:31.154 --> 00:06:31.775
ambassadors,

00:06:32.055 --> 00:06:32.916
provincial governors,

00:06:33.196 --> 00:06:36.960
and parliamentary officials who rise at dawn to be present at the grand awakening.

00:06:37.561 --> 00:06:39.142
One must be there to hear the news,

00:06:39.623 --> 00:06:40.143
to gossip,

00:06:40.423 --> 00:06:41.224
but more importantly,

00:06:41.865 --> 00:06:42.465
to be seen.

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:52.453
Louis XIV has chosen the self-portrait of Van Dyck to hang above his door.

00:06:53.473 --> 00:06:55.755
High on the walls he has placed paintings by Valentin,

00:06:56.175 --> 00:06:57.896
the French pupil of Caravaggio.

00:06:59.718 --> 00:07:00.958
He likes Valentin's work.

00:07:01.799 --> 00:07:02.760
The tribute to Caesar.

00:07:04.081 --> 00:07:04.641
Saint John.

00:07:06.943 --> 00:07:07.543
Saint Matthew.

00:07:10.805 --> 00:07:11.386
Saint Mark.

00:07:17.642 --> 00:07:19.983
Once the ritual of the Grand Awakening is completed,

00:07:20.343 --> 00:07:22.803
the king meets with his counselors in the adjoining room,

00:07:23.224 --> 00:07:24.904
the official royal office.

00:07:25.444 --> 00:07:26.484
In the mid-18th century,

00:07:26.845 --> 00:07:29.205
Louis XV completely transformed this room,

00:07:29.685 --> 00:07:30.366
but nevertheless,

00:07:30.706 --> 00:07:33.206
it has always been the reigning heart of the realm,

00:07:33.867 --> 00:07:36.247
the symbol of the scepter and hand of justice.

00:07:42.109 --> 00:07:42.989
For more than a century,

00:07:43.369 --> 00:07:45.930
from 1682 until 1789,

00:07:46.310 --> 00:07:50.093
Versailles is the seat of absolute monarchy and the government of France.

00:07:56.278 --> 00:07:57.919
The king rules alone.

00:07:58.659 --> 00:08:01.561
He demands consistency and obedience from his ministers.

00:08:05.544 --> 00:08:06.105
In this room,

00:08:06.645 --> 00:08:08.487
every day he summons his counselors,

00:08:08.847 --> 00:08:12.390
signs documents and receives petitions both grand and humble.

00:08:13.122 --> 00:08:15.924
It is the place where his subjects may address him directly.

00:08:21.267 --> 00:08:21.987
But sometimes,

00:08:22.447 --> 00:08:23.648
instead of his counselors,

00:08:24.208 --> 00:08:27.950
the king summons his superintendent of buildings and his architect,

00:08:28.711 --> 00:08:31.252
because the king's ruling passion is building.

00:08:32.953 --> 00:08:36.495
Versailles was originally a hunting lodge built by Louis XIII,

00:08:37.476 --> 00:08:41.398
but it would be unthinkable to keep such a primitive dwelling as the royal residence.

00:08:42.026 --> 00:08:43.467
It must be completely refurbished,

00:08:43.787 --> 00:08:44.427
terraces built,

00:08:44.787 --> 00:08:45.588
marshes drained,

00:08:45.928 --> 00:08:46.928
nature subdued.

00:08:47.829 --> 00:08:49.929
Construction will last more than 50 years.

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:51.110
At its height,

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:53.891
more than 15,000 men will be employed.

00:08:54.952 --> 00:08:55.292
At first,

00:08:55.293 --> 00:09:01.915
the young king merely embellishes the existing house of cards and visits it with female companions from time to time.

00:09:02.815 --> 00:09:03.475
In the forecourt,

00:09:03.855 --> 00:09:06.316
the architect Leveaux constructs the common rooms,

00:09:06.857 --> 00:09:07.937
stables to the left,

00:09:08.377 --> 00:09:09.378
kitchens to the right.

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:10.731
The gardens,

00:09:11.111 --> 00:09:13.253
following the layout of the gardener Le Nôtre,

00:09:13.854 --> 00:09:14.935
advance at a pace.

00:09:15.835 --> 00:09:16.916
When he is 30 years old,

00:09:17.197 --> 00:09:21.201
Louis XIV tells Colbert that he wants to move his council to Versailles.

00:09:21.881 --> 00:09:24.644
This prompts the first construction to enlarge the castle.

00:09:25.745 --> 00:09:26.866
On the side facing the town,

00:09:27.126 --> 00:09:29.268
Le Vaux's common rooms are linked up to the castle,

00:09:29.549 --> 00:09:32.151
and their facades are soon adorned with columns,

00:09:32.672 --> 00:09:33.212
balconies,

00:09:33.813 --> 00:09:34.393
statues.

00:09:44.654 --> 00:09:47.975
The original castle is literally surrounded by new buildings.

00:09:48.656 --> 00:09:52.537
The first idea is to enclose it on the park side with two lateral wings,

00:09:52.938 --> 00:09:54.358
linked by a huge terrace.

00:09:54.918 --> 00:09:57.560
The influence of the Italian Baroque villa is dominant,

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:01.861
but the uncertain French climate makes this terrace impractical.

00:10:02.582 --> 00:10:03.662
It soon disappears.

00:10:04.763 --> 00:10:06.743
The Hall of Mirrors takes its place.

00:10:08.024 --> 00:10:09.024
Though the terrace is gone,

00:10:09.525 --> 00:10:12.186
the architecture stays predominantly Baroque.

00:10:13.166 --> 00:10:16.428
The ground floor is designed like a huge pedestal in bas-relief.

00:10:17.429 --> 00:10:20.331
The first or noble floor is adorned with columns,

00:10:21.112 --> 00:10:22.833
statues resting on cornices,

00:10:23.774 --> 00:10:28.818
flat roofs disguised by a huge balustrade bristling with urns spouting flame,

00:10:29.839 --> 00:10:30.259
and trophies.

00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:34.943
In 1682,

00:10:35.483 --> 00:10:38.406
Louis XIV makes Versailles his official residence.

00:10:39.146 --> 00:10:40.047
In only ten years,

00:10:40.548 --> 00:10:42.149
Jules-Adouin Monsart,

00:10:42.570 --> 00:10:43.831
the king's new head architect,

00:10:44.311 --> 00:10:46.894
expands the building's surface fivefold.

00:10:48.996 --> 00:10:51.138
He adds two immense wings to the castle.

00:10:51.878 --> 00:10:55.162
The wing facing the park is designed for the comfort of the royal family.

00:10:55.982 --> 00:10:57.143
The wing facing the town,

00:10:57.724 --> 00:10:59.626
for the discomfort of the courtiers.

00:11:01.203 --> 00:11:01.684
And of course,

00:11:01.964 --> 00:11:04.747
the middle is reserved for the royal apartments.

00:11:11.453 --> 00:11:12.554
The king's apartments.

00:11:13.455 --> 00:11:13.655
This,

00:11:13.996 --> 00:11:15.117
the domain of the sun,

00:11:15.677 --> 00:11:18.780
the domain of a young king who wants to dazzle the world.

00:11:20.142 --> 00:11:25.147
Solar emblems intertwined with royal insignias in an explosion of gold.

00:11:28.331 --> 00:11:31.373
Cornucopias overflow with the bounty of the great century.

00:11:32.794 --> 00:11:33.955
A wealth of marble,

00:11:34.495 --> 00:11:35.596
a brilliance of color,

00:11:36.797 --> 00:11:37.257
grandeur,

00:11:38.298 --> 00:11:38.578
light,

00:11:39.719 --> 00:11:40.099
harmony.

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:42.921
It has been said of Le Brun,

00:11:43.201 --> 00:11:44.562
the creator of this decoration,

00:11:45.322 --> 00:11:47.204
all the arts were his servants.

00:11:48.364 --> 00:11:49.445
Marble becomes a painting,

00:11:50.486 --> 00:11:51.326
sculptures too.

00:11:53.139 --> 00:11:55.020
Just as the sun is the symbol of the king,

00:11:55.681 --> 00:11:57.802
each room is dedicated to a planet.

00:11:59.423 --> 00:12:00.404
In the middle of this room,

00:12:00.904 --> 00:12:01.265
Venus,

00:12:01.405 --> 00:12:02.425
the goddess of love.

00:12:03.826 --> 00:12:05.427
On the arch above the door,

00:12:06.068 --> 00:12:11.211
the newlyweds Alexander and Roxanne are a reminder of the glory of the king's own wedding.

00:12:12.032 --> 00:12:15.134
The metaphoric message is clear to Louis XIV's subjects.

00:12:21.387 --> 00:12:24.249
Passing from room to room is a mythological journey.

00:12:32.896 --> 00:12:33.756
Diana's Salon.

00:12:34.697 --> 00:12:37.699
Diana is Apollo's sister and the goddess of the hunt.

00:12:40.821 --> 00:12:44.604
This bust of Louis XIV is a masterpiece by Bernini,

00:12:45.325 --> 00:12:47.406
the most renowned sculptor of the time.

00:12:49.399 --> 00:12:51.501
In 1665,

00:12:51.502 --> 00:12:55.064
Louis XIV agreed to pose for this finely crafted sculpture.

00:12:55.845 --> 00:12:57.726
He was 27 years old at the time.

00:12:59.908 --> 00:13:03.111
Lorenzo Benigni had the opportunity to get to know the young monarch,

00:13:03.612 --> 00:13:05.513
whose taste he considered questionable.

00:13:06.594 --> 00:13:13.781
The Italian artists thought that the French king placed too much importance on fussy detail and paid too little attention on the grand picture,

00:13:14.622 --> 00:13:15.562
the Italian style.

00:13:17.151 --> 00:13:20.112
The royal apartment shows how the king's taste evolved.

00:13:22.132 --> 00:13:23.033
In Diana's salon,

00:13:23.393 --> 00:13:24.373
there is a billiard table.

00:13:25.293 --> 00:13:27.594
Louis XIV is an excellent billiards player.

00:13:28.654 --> 00:13:29.995
Three evenings a week in the winter,

00:13:30.095 --> 00:13:31.175
between six and ten,

00:13:31.675 --> 00:13:34.916
he demonstrates his prowess before an admiring court.

00:13:36.236 --> 00:13:36.917
Mars Salon.

00:13:37.777 --> 00:13:40.718
The largest room in the king's quarters is the ballroom.

00:13:41.638 --> 00:13:45.239
Here the king's retinue dance to the sound of violins and oboes.

00:13:46.003 --> 00:13:47.004
In the middle of the ceiling,

00:13:47.804 --> 00:13:48.204
Mars,

00:13:48.685 --> 00:13:49.525
the god of war,

00:13:49.966 --> 00:13:51.847
sits in a chariot drawn by wolves.

00:13:52.667 --> 00:13:55.129
This room is situated in the line of formal rooms.

00:13:55.969 --> 00:13:56.350
Every day,

00:13:56.690 --> 00:14:00.372
courtiers and those curious to see the royal art collections stroll through it.

00:14:01.052 --> 00:14:04.194
A printed guide helps them to identify the paintings and sculptures.

00:14:05.615 --> 00:14:06.896
King David playing the harp,

00:14:07.376 --> 00:14:08.317
painted by a Dominican,

00:14:08.717 --> 00:14:10.558
was Louis XIV's favorite canvas.

00:14:15.571 --> 00:14:18.493
The Pilgrims at Emmaus by the Venetian master Veronese,

00:14:18.974 --> 00:14:21.096
one of the most sought-after artists of his time.

00:14:22.096 --> 00:14:26.600
Darius's Family at Alexander's Feet by the king's master painter Charles Le Brun.

00:14:27.721 --> 00:14:29.803
The message is clear for those initiated.

00:14:30.563 --> 00:14:34.487
New French painting has achieved equality with the greatest of Italian art.

00:14:50.370 --> 00:14:55.992
The automatic clock in the Mercury Salon is all that remains of the furniture from the Louis XIV period.

00:14:56.632 --> 00:14:59.633
It was a gift of the clockmaker himself in 1706.

00:15:05.295 --> 00:15:05.775
Every hour,

00:15:06.035 --> 00:15:07.895
a small statue of the king appears,

00:15:08.215 --> 00:15:10.976
and an angel descends to crown him with a laurel wreath.

00:15:30.533 --> 00:15:31.033
Originally,

00:15:31.113 --> 00:15:32.574
this room was the royal chamber.

00:15:33.554 --> 00:15:34.775
It was dedicated to Apollo,

00:15:35.195 --> 00:15:35.915
God of the Sun,

00:15:36.475 --> 00:15:38.856
the only heavenly body that rises and sets.

00:15:39.936 --> 00:15:40.316
Naturally,

00:15:40.656 --> 00:15:45.698
this is the room where Louis XIV's Apollonian awakenings and reposings were acted out.

00:15:47.238 --> 00:15:51.340
Charles de Lafosse's use of color is particularly well demonstrated here.

00:15:53.981 --> 00:15:54.541
In the corners,

00:15:54.761 --> 00:15:58.543
he paints the four continents bathed in the nourishing rays of the heavens.

00:16:02.684 --> 00:16:03.305
In the arching,

00:16:03.705 --> 00:16:05.426
Vespasian builds the Colosseum.

00:16:07.527 --> 00:16:10.488
Augustus has the port of Mycenae excavated.

00:16:11.608 --> 00:16:15.370
Thus Louis XIV shows the world his talents as a builder.

00:16:16.734 --> 00:16:18.755
This portrait of the king in his coronation robes,

00:16:19.195 --> 00:16:19.995
painted by Rigaud,

00:16:20.576 --> 00:16:26.698
will hang in the palace throughout the 18th century as a permanent tribute to the greatness of Louis the Builder.

00:16:30.580 --> 00:16:34.802
The message implied by the decor changes and intensifies in the war gallery.

00:16:35.782 --> 00:16:39.144
The glories of battle are no longer disguised in mythological trappings.

00:16:39.944 --> 00:16:41.185
No longer do we see Apollo,

00:16:41.585 --> 00:16:43.826
but Louis XIV himself ravaging the enemy.

00:16:44.830 --> 00:16:46.751
At the height of his reign in 1678,

00:16:47.091 --> 00:16:50.533
the Treaty of Nimegon seals his victory in the war with Holland.

00:16:52.014 --> 00:16:54.635
Nothing is untouched by this mood of exultation.

00:16:55.575 --> 00:16:56.036
Mirrors,

00:16:56.796 --> 00:16:57.696
rare marbles,

00:16:58.317 --> 00:16:59.377
superbly chiseled,

00:17:00.038 --> 00:17:01.198
and gilded bronzes.

00:17:03.359 --> 00:17:04.080
The War Gallery,

00:17:04.720 --> 00:17:05.620
the Hall of Mirrors,

00:17:06.281 --> 00:17:06.781
and finally,

00:17:07.041 --> 00:17:07.862
the Peace Gallery.

00:17:09.102 --> 00:17:14.224
More than 100 glittering yards immortalize the majesty of the king's apartments.

00:17:16.385 --> 00:17:17.045
Most of the time,

00:17:17.425 --> 00:17:19.746
the great hall of mirrors is a kind of waiting room,

00:17:20.506 --> 00:17:31.610
but sometimes it is the stage for galas given for princely weddings or perhaps receptions for ambassadors from distant lands who will return home to their sovereigns with tales of the grandeur of the king of France.

00:17:59.299 --> 00:17:59.419
Le

00:17:59.459 --> 00:18:04.542
Brun reserved the arched ceiling for work celebrating the greatest events of Louis XIV's reign.

00:18:05.563 --> 00:18:09.285
They revolve around a central composition showing the great powers.

00:18:10.206 --> 00:18:10.867
In their presence,

00:18:11.247 --> 00:18:16.971
the young king turns away from light-hearted dalliance to contemplate the immortal crown.

00:18:24.737 --> 00:18:31.302
Seventeen mirrored arcades are an arrogant display at a time when mirrors are an item most can ill afford.

00:18:32.303 --> 00:18:36.386
The technical prowess of the kingdom's new factories is the envy of all Europe.

00:18:41.882 --> 00:18:43.483
Mirrors of glass inside,

00:18:44.424 --> 00:18:46.345
mirrors of water outside.

00:18:57.212 --> 00:19:02.976
Great ornamental pools lined with allegorical statues of the rivers of France typify

00:19:03.036 --> 00:19:05.317
these gardens of high Baroque style.

00:19:14.119 --> 00:19:17.882
Versailles has the most magnificent royal garden in all of Europe.

00:19:18.803 --> 00:19:19.344
Its creator,

00:19:19.804 --> 00:19:20.585
André Le Nôtre,

00:19:20.925 --> 00:19:22.166
is not merely a gardener.

00:19:22.966 --> 00:19:24.568
He is a landscape architect,

00:19:25.088 --> 00:19:26.069
perhaps the first.

00:19:27.010 --> 00:19:28.291
He transforms nature,

00:19:28.811 --> 00:19:29.492
organizes it,

00:19:30.172 --> 00:19:30.913
expands it.

00:19:38.699 --> 00:19:40.820
But Versailles is also a pleasure garden,

00:19:41.621 --> 00:19:43.422
which never ceases to amaze.

00:19:46.083 --> 00:19:47.264
Sculptures and dogs'

00:19:47.284 --> 00:19:50.465
mouths spouting water reflect the greatest pleasure of the court,

00:19:51.026 --> 00:19:51.466
the hunt.

00:19:57.009 --> 00:19:59.470
The king would sometimes give his own guided tours,

00:19:59.851 --> 00:20:00.951
setting the itinerary,

00:20:01.271 --> 00:20:03.332
where to stop to admire a particular view,

00:20:03.993 --> 00:20:07.435
when all the joy of discovery finally overwhelms the senses.

00:20:12.675 --> 00:20:13.395
The pâtères,

00:20:13.956 --> 00:20:15.196
exposed to sun and wind,

00:20:15.557 --> 00:20:17.538
are not really intended for strolls,

00:20:18.078 --> 00:20:19.499
but for enhancing the buildings.

00:20:20.399 --> 00:20:24.081
They can be admired from the apartments and crossed to reach the orangerie.

00:20:24.082 --> 00:20:31.445
1080 exotic tropical trees.

00:20:32.386 --> 00:20:33.547
Orange trees from Portugal,

00:20:33.827 --> 00:20:34.687
Spain and Italy.

00:20:35.428 --> 00:20:36.028
Lemon trees,

00:20:36.468 --> 00:20:38.209
oleanders and pomegranates.

00:20:38.830 --> 00:20:41.011
Some of them over two centuries old.

00:20:44.367 --> 00:20:45.048
The Orangerie,

00:20:45.568 --> 00:20:46.830
with its purity of line,

00:20:47.150 --> 00:20:47.911
use of space,

00:20:48.391 --> 00:20:48.511
its

00:20:48.972 --> 00:20:49.973
13-meter high arches,

00:20:50.594 --> 00:20:52.996
reaffirms the genius of Adouin-Monsart.

00:20:54.157 --> 00:20:58.562
Its southern exposure and double windows kept the Orangerie at a constant temperature,

00:20:59.043 --> 00:21:00.524
5 to 8 degrees centigrade,

00:21:01.185 --> 00:21:02.026
even during the winter.

00:21:17.895 --> 00:21:18.536
To the left,

00:21:18.776 --> 00:21:19.497
facing west,

00:21:20.077 --> 00:21:26.404
a dozen groves have been concealed in the woods that stretch to the north and south of this vast perspective.

00:21:29.548 --> 00:21:32.731
An outdoor ballroom in the bowl of an amphitheater.

00:21:33.712 --> 00:21:37.596
Delicate cascades and filigree rockwork adorned with shells.

00:21:43.459 --> 00:21:45.340
The court dances in this grove.

00:21:46.600 --> 00:21:55.103
The time has now passed when the young sun king would shine in the company of such mistresses as the gentle Lavalière or the exuberant Athénaïs,

00:21:55.563 --> 00:21:56.783
marquise de Montespan.

00:21:57.964 --> 00:22:00.925
These enchanted gardens rang with the music of Lully,

00:22:01.365 --> 00:22:02.685
the poetry of Molière,

00:22:03.306 --> 00:22:05.686
and forged the reputation of Versailles.

00:22:10.595 --> 00:22:16.039
The secondary alleys that run parallel to the vast perspective are dedicated to the seasons,

00:22:16.540 --> 00:22:16.660
with,

00:22:16.680 --> 00:22:17.040
of course,

00:22:17.080 --> 00:22:17.440
Apollo,

00:22:17.721 --> 00:22:18.241
sun god,

00:22:18.641 --> 00:22:19.722
reigning from the center.

00:22:22.204 --> 00:22:23.785
The play of light and shadow,

00:22:24.526 --> 00:22:26.067
one hour melting into the next,

00:22:26.487 --> 00:22:27.929
the year melting away.

00:22:29.109 --> 00:22:30.190
God of the grape harvest,

00:22:30.531 --> 00:22:30.971
Bacchus,

00:22:31.712 --> 00:22:33.193
bails the September waters,

00:22:33.793 --> 00:22:35.034
and autumn rains.

00:22:37.907 --> 00:22:39.608
Further down the path cut through the woods,

00:22:40.108 --> 00:22:40.908
the god of winter,

00:22:41.349 --> 00:22:41.729
Saturn,

00:22:42.269 --> 00:22:44.210
contemplates clouds while cherubs,

00:22:44.530 --> 00:22:45.810
the promise of spring to come,

00:22:46.371 --> 00:22:47.191
flutter about him.

00:22:48.351 --> 00:22:50.812
These fountains are made of gilded and painted lead.

00:22:57.775 --> 00:23:01.677
The gardener has created a sense of intimacy with winding paths,

00:23:02.057 --> 00:23:04.138
secret spots hidden behind trellis work.

00:23:21.615 --> 00:23:25.217
The peristeel of the colonnade is next to the marronnier's room.

00:23:30.100 --> 00:23:32.722
Here in this space used exclusively for concerts,

00:23:33.162 --> 00:23:35.904
we see pure classic lines with sculpture,

00:23:36.424 --> 00:23:36.884
marble,

00:23:37.645 --> 00:23:38.045
water.

00:23:40.126 --> 00:23:42.928
The Rape of Persephone by Pluto by François Girardon,

00:23:43.228 --> 00:23:44.389
the king's favorite sculptor,

00:23:44.809 --> 00:23:46.250
thrusts upward from the center.

00:23:48.203 --> 00:23:54.248
This is the perfect marriage of the architecture of Jules-Adoua Mansart and the gardens conceived by Le Nôtre.

00:24:16.727 --> 00:24:19.788
At the heart of the garden is the fountain of Apollo's chariot.

00:24:20.989 --> 00:24:25.651
The young god is drawn by four fiery horses as he rises from the dawn.

00:24:26.812 --> 00:24:30.693
He is escorted by four tritons and by four sea monsters,

00:24:31.574 --> 00:24:32.534
four of everything.

00:24:44.591 --> 00:24:46.993
The solar theme dominates in the whole garden,

00:24:47.733 --> 00:24:50.556
but it's particularly apparent in the central axis.

00:24:51.737 --> 00:24:52.537
The four seasons,

00:24:53.038 --> 00:24:53.858
the four continents,

00:24:54.199 --> 00:24:55.480
the four natures of man.

00:24:56.240 --> 00:24:58.882
The light bearer creates an entire cosmology.

00:25:02.425 --> 00:25:04.547
Apollonian myth is rich with tales.

00:25:05.098 --> 00:25:05.738
his wars,

00:25:05.898 --> 00:25:06.439
his loves,

00:25:06.479 --> 00:25:07.339
his pastimes.

00:25:08.179 --> 00:25:10.920
Latona's fountain shows a scene from the god's childhood.

00:25:12.041 --> 00:25:13.942
To flee the wrath of Jupiter's wife Juno,

00:25:14.482 --> 00:25:14.882
Latona,

00:25:15.322 --> 00:25:16.623
Diana and Apollo's mother,

00:25:17.203 --> 00:25:21.625
seek shelter in a foreign land where she is cruelly tormented by the local peasants.

00:25:22.525 --> 00:25:22.905
Desperate,

00:25:23.245 --> 00:25:24.926
she begs the father of her children,

00:25:25.246 --> 00:25:25.986
Lord of Olympus,

00:25:26.226 --> 00:25:26.847
for protection.

00:25:27.967 --> 00:25:32.629
Jupiter transforms the insolent farmers into toads and lizards.

00:25:53.678 --> 00:25:57.301
You cannot see the grove of Apollo's baths from Latona's fountain.

00:25:58.562 --> 00:26:04.908
Louis XVI would ask Hubert Robert to completely redesign the grotto of this remarkable sculptural trio,

00:26:05.849 --> 00:26:07.630
originally commissioned by Louis XIV.

00:26:07.731 --> 00:26:07.851
The

00:26:18.640 --> 00:26:19.441
Sun Horses

00:26:40.374 --> 00:26:42.255
Awaited on by his nymphs.

00:26:44.256 --> 00:26:48.098
This group illustrates the god bathing after his daily toils.

00:26:49.760 --> 00:26:55.603
François Girardon and Thomas Régnodin carve these seven figures from the finest white marble.

00:27:06.446 --> 00:27:08.047
The huge Grand Canal.

00:27:09.127 --> 00:27:10.927
An entire flotilla sails here.

00:27:11.648 --> 00:27:12.028
Sloops,

00:27:12.388 --> 00:27:13.408
Venetian gondolas,

00:27:14.028 --> 00:27:15.829
yachts presented by the King of England,

00:27:16.369 --> 00:27:17.389
and royal galleys.

00:27:19.670 --> 00:27:22.591
And deep in the forests lie the hunting paths.

00:27:35.746 --> 00:27:37.727
As Versailles expands more and more,

00:27:38.387 --> 00:27:40.708
the king finds he needs a new country house,

00:27:41.528 --> 00:27:42.108
the Trianon.

00:27:47.450 --> 00:27:49.090
He can invite his family here.

00:27:50.011 --> 00:27:50.631
Both his heir,

00:27:50.671 --> 00:27:51.391
the Monseigneur,

00:27:52.071 --> 00:27:54.872
and his many mistress's children visit him here often.

00:27:56.192 --> 00:27:57.093
Madame de Montaigneau,

00:27:57.933 --> 00:27:59.233
whom he had secretly married,

00:27:59.494 --> 00:27:59.934
joins them.

00:28:01.078 --> 00:28:03.640
She tells of how one summer in 1689,

00:28:04.101 --> 00:28:10.726
the perfume from the flowers was so overwhelming that the party had to flee the garden before all the ladies fainted.

00:28:14.309 --> 00:28:14.970
At the triennal,

00:28:15.971 --> 00:28:19.153
all is organized to make the garden an extension of the house.

00:28:20.634 --> 00:28:21.735
There is only one floor.

00:28:22.636 --> 00:28:24.237
The court is seen from the garden.

00:28:26.518 --> 00:28:26.958
Inside,

00:28:27.459 --> 00:28:30.921
every angle can be contemplated through long bay windows.

00:28:32.862 --> 00:28:35.384
The gardens are visible everywhere.

00:28:36.604 --> 00:28:38.365
It is a palace of flowers.

00:28:56.170 --> 00:28:56.970
Leaving the Triennale,

00:28:57.491 --> 00:28:59.972
one can regain the palace by way of the north wing.

00:29:06.494 --> 00:29:08.375
Everything is a celebration to water.

00:29:09.056 --> 00:29:11.897
The sparkling fountains seem to shape the light around them.

00:29:19.362 --> 00:29:21.304
At the top of the Alley of the Infants,

00:29:22.004 --> 00:29:24.606
one can see the nymph's bath and the pyramid.

00:29:26.508 --> 00:29:28.509
Behind this fountain of crustaceans,

00:29:29.130 --> 00:29:30.431
dolphins and tritons,

00:29:30.971 --> 00:29:32.312
the chapel soars.

00:29:33.333 --> 00:29:37.276
It is fitting that the house of God stand higher than the house of the king.

00:29:39.358 --> 00:29:40.699
Angels bearing palms,

00:29:41.059 --> 00:29:41.539
cherubs,

00:29:41.980 --> 00:29:43.821
fleur-de-lis line the roof.

00:29:55.426 --> 00:29:57.834
The chapel is as luminous as heaven itself.

00:29:58.636 --> 00:30:01.966
It is the manifestation of the triumph of Christ the King.

00:30:04.750 --> 00:30:06.151
Like all Palladian chapels,

00:30:06.611 --> 00:30:07.832
it has a second floor.

00:30:12.795 --> 00:30:15.377
The pillars look like embroidery set in stone.

00:30:16.318 --> 00:30:16.698
Above them,

00:30:16.838 --> 00:30:20.761
angels carrying the symbols of Christ's passion mark the stations of the cross.

00:30:21.681 --> 00:30:27.585
This procession of pain leads to the altar just as the passion led to death before the resurrection.

00:30:29.338 --> 00:30:29.638
Death,

00:30:30.339 --> 00:30:31.019
the deposition,

00:30:31.940 --> 00:30:36.302
Christ after the crucifixion held in the arms of John and mourned by the Virgin Mary.

00:30:37.583 --> 00:30:41.406
This gilded bronze bas-relief is the masterwork of Van Cleve.

00:30:46.269 --> 00:30:49.651
But the great sacrifice is followed by the triumph of the resurrection.

00:30:50.531 --> 00:30:51.772
It is not Apollo's son,

00:30:51.912 --> 00:30:54.194
but God's son that illuminates the earth.

00:30:55.138 --> 00:30:57.379
Yahweh can be read in the triangle,

00:30:58.140 --> 00:30:59.501
the symbol of the Holy Trinity.

00:31:02.742 --> 00:31:08.205
Every day chords swell from the giant organ as the prayer for the monarch lifts heavenward.

00:31:08.906 --> 00:31:10.907
Domini salvum fac regem,

00:31:11.647 --> 00:31:12.828
God save our King.

00:31:18.391 --> 00:31:22.994
The domed ceiling shows a sky torn asunder by the glory of a reborn Christ.

00:31:23.578 --> 00:31:24.579
Master of the Universe.

00:31:25.560 --> 00:31:40.437
The painting is by Lafosse.

00:31:41.558 --> 00:31:44.041
The Eternal Father by Antoine Coipel.

00:31:52.262 --> 00:31:52.862
And finally,

00:31:53.102 --> 00:31:53.602
the dove,

00:31:54.303 --> 00:31:55.663
the third element of the Trinity,

00:31:56.223 --> 00:31:57.004
by Jean Jouvenet.

00:31:58.124 --> 00:32:02.245
The Holy Spirit descends to the royal balcony with the very Christian king,

00:32:02.726 --> 00:32:03.546
the sacred king,

00:32:04.226 --> 00:32:05.407
lieutenant of God on earth,

00:32:06.067 --> 00:32:07.187
receives mass daily.

00:32:09.948 --> 00:32:12.209
On the floor below is the cipher of Saint Louis,

00:32:12.829 --> 00:32:14.810
forefather and patron saint of the monarchy.

00:32:19.678 --> 00:32:22.819
This chapel is the last construction of Louis XIV's reign.

00:32:23.740 --> 00:32:27.261
It was begun by Ardoin-Monsart and completed by his brother-in-law,

00:32:27.661 --> 00:32:28.522
Robert de Cotte.

00:32:29.662 --> 00:32:31.603
It is a huge sculptural undertaking,

00:32:32.403 --> 00:32:38.085
the work of three generations of artists and workers laboring for a reign that would never end.

00:32:40.126 --> 00:32:42.407
The chapel is consecrated in 1710.

00:32:43.468 --> 00:32:44.228
Five years later,

00:32:44.768 --> 00:32:47.029
the Sun King breathes his last.

00:32:49.618 --> 00:32:51.640
Louis XIV's great-grandson Louis XV,

00:32:52.180 --> 00:32:52.760
the beloved,

00:32:53.241 --> 00:32:54.562
painted by François Lemoyne.

00:32:55.562 --> 00:32:56.903
Nothing seems to have changed.

00:32:57.624 --> 00:32:58.224
The young king,

00:32:58.645 --> 00:32:59.985
most beautiful man of the realm,

00:33:00.366 --> 00:33:02.427
is also depicted as a Roman emperor.

00:33:02.988 --> 00:33:03.528
But this time,

00:33:04.028 --> 00:33:05.630
he's holding out an olive branch.

00:33:08.494 --> 00:33:11.577
Louis XV continues his great-grandfather's building projects,

00:33:12.137 --> 00:33:13.538
especially Neptune's fountain.

00:33:14.619 --> 00:33:19.424
The new fountains are extravagantly rococo and add a new flavor to the garden's aspect.

00:33:24.368 --> 00:33:25.869
But even at Versailles,

00:33:25.870 --> 00:33:26.630
change is coming.

00:33:27.711 --> 00:33:31.734
A libertine mood from Paris brings a sense of folly and intimacy.

00:33:32.915 --> 00:33:35.557
And Louis XV is a man of his time.

00:33:46.498 --> 00:34:05.061
large spaces shrink and here we find the king in his small apartment the king lives in a private room now this is where he sleeps after enduring the rituals of awakening and reposing in the chilly formal halls of louis the fourteenth

00:34:09.254 --> 00:34:10.535
In 1754,

00:34:10.975 --> 00:34:13.536
the Great Antechamber becomes the office of the clock,

00:34:14.117 --> 00:34:16.858
an homage to this monument of science and art.

00:34:25.163 --> 00:34:29.025
This astronomical clock shows the phases of the moon,

00:34:30.726 --> 00:34:37.250
the days up to the last second of the year 9,999,

00:34:37.251 --> 00:34:37.730
the hour.

00:34:43.426 --> 00:34:43.946
And finally,

00:34:44.586 --> 00:34:45.567
in a crystal globe,

00:34:46.407 --> 00:34:48.168
it shows the planetary movements.

00:34:56.430 --> 00:34:59.371
The molding along the ceiling is delicate and fanciful.

00:35:00.152 --> 00:35:01.152
It is pure Rococo.

00:35:02.393 --> 00:35:03.393
Throughout Versailles,

00:35:03.813 --> 00:35:05.994
elegance is giving way to daring.

00:35:13.294 --> 00:35:16.135
The king's desk is perhaps the most famous in the world.

00:35:17.256 --> 00:35:21.798
Louis XV wanted to leave his papers in disarray unseen by prying eyes.

00:35:22.598 --> 00:35:23.418
The cabinetmaker,

00:35:23.758 --> 00:35:24.219
Resnais,

00:35:24.759 --> 00:35:27.020
invented for him the first roll-top desk.

00:35:27.760 --> 00:35:28.660
It took him ten years,

00:35:29.001 --> 00:35:29.581
and he signed it

00:35:29.961 --> 00:35:31.842
Resnais 1769,

00:35:32.502 --> 00:35:33.682
which is almost unheard of.

00:35:36.664 --> 00:35:38.484
The skill of the inlay is astonishing,

00:35:39.045 --> 00:35:41.506
as is the bronze work and the elegance of line.

00:35:42.894 --> 00:35:43.914
The tiny statues,

00:35:44.875 --> 00:35:46.055
the attention to detail,

00:35:59.659 --> 00:36:04.381
the keyhole is shaped like a flue de lys with a matching key showing the royal cipher.

00:36:05.181 --> 00:36:07.582
A quarter turn reveals the secret drawers.

00:36:15.838 --> 00:36:19.560
One by one Louis XV received his ministers in this small office.

00:36:20.461 --> 00:36:21.121
While next door,

00:36:21.681 --> 00:36:26.704
the spies of the king's secret service awaited orders to scour Europe in the sovereign's name.

00:36:28.245 --> 00:36:32.068
The metal cabinet by Gaudreau is all curve upon curve,

00:36:32.588 --> 00:36:35.089
a perfect example of Rococo predominance in the

00:36:35.950 --> 00:36:36.070
1740s.

00:36:37.411 --> 00:36:38.131
Thirty years later,

00:36:38.671 --> 00:36:42.294
two other more sober corner cabinets were made to complement it.

00:36:43.402 --> 00:36:46.726
They held the newly minted medals of the history of the realm.

00:36:55.836 --> 00:36:58.039
As in all these perfectly detailed rooms,

00:36:58.539 --> 00:37:03.725
the carved marble of the fireplace is perfectly coordinated with that of the mantelpiece.

00:37:13.258 --> 00:37:14.138
In Adelaide's room,

00:37:14.478 --> 00:37:16.579
we find some of the best woodwork in the castle,

00:37:16.999 --> 00:37:17.820
made by Webeck.

00:37:18.920 --> 00:37:19.120
Here,

00:37:19.180 --> 00:37:20.701
Louis XV's favorite daughter,

00:37:21.141 --> 00:37:22.561
the third of his seven girls,

00:37:23.182 --> 00:37:26.463
studies Italian with Goldoni and the harp with Beaumarchais.

00:37:27.763 --> 00:37:34.626
This is undoubtedly where the prodigy Mozart played harpsichord for the royal family in 1763.

00:37:48.706 --> 00:37:49.146
Combs,

00:37:49.426 --> 00:37:49.767
scissors,

00:37:49.787 --> 00:37:53.568
and sponge are reminders that this little room was the king's bathroom.

00:37:54.729 --> 00:37:56.870
The delicacy of these gilded designs,

00:37:57.510 --> 00:37:58.190
green gold,

00:37:58.650 --> 00:37:59.331
matte gold,

00:37:59.731 --> 00:38:00.471
glittering gold,

00:38:01.312 --> 00:38:05.273
showed that private comforts and pleasures were integral to this king's reign.

00:38:08.495 --> 00:38:09.455
At the heart of the castle,

00:38:09.815 --> 00:38:13.277
the stag's court opens on what looks like a stack of little rooms,

00:38:13.917 --> 00:38:14.758
the king's suite.

00:38:22.822 --> 00:38:27.006
A graceful stairway opens onto a landing that leads to the most intimate rooms.

00:38:31.109 --> 00:38:31.810
Low ceilings,

00:38:32.431 --> 00:38:35.293
undecorated walls very different from the preceding reign.

00:38:36.594 --> 00:38:39.497
The king's domestic life is hidden even from his courtiers.

00:38:44.541 --> 00:38:46.223
Surrounded by ambition and intrigues,

00:38:46.663 --> 00:38:49.826
Louis XV turns in on himself and his private life.

00:38:50.866 --> 00:38:51.427
After the hunt,

00:38:51.787 --> 00:38:54.668
he summons a few select friends for a quiet supper,

00:38:55.529 --> 00:38:59.631
regaled by brilliant conversation and France's finest cuisine.

00:39:09.456 --> 00:39:10.997
The king is at ease here.

00:39:12.238 --> 00:39:14.699
He can be free to relax in the company of friends.

00:39:15.800 --> 00:39:19.262
The king submits less and less to the stiff ritual of the public meal.

00:39:19.970 --> 00:39:22.572
And no one is ever quite sure where he is.

00:39:23.813 --> 00:39:26.055
Perhaps he is bantering with Madame de Pompadour,

00:39:26.715 --> 00:39:28.957
the queen of this little world for 20 years.

00:39:38.504 --> 00:39:41.566
The king's last great passion would be for the young beauty

00:39:41.926 --> 00:39:42.707
Madame du Barry.

00:39:43.888 --> 00:39:46.490
Rumor has it that she is the daughter of a priest.

00:39:48.070 --> 00:39:51.492
The court is outraged when this young countess is installed with the king.

00:39:51.512 --> 00:39:56.975
1770.

00:39:57.776 --> 00:40:00.197
The court discovers the opera house when the dauphin,

00:40:00.577 --> 00:40:01.678
the future Louis XVI,

00:40:02.439 --> 00:40:03.639
weds Marie-Antoinette.

00:40:05.080 --> 00:40:07.622
By using old plans from the Louis XIV period,

00:40:08.042 --> 00:40:11.104
the architect Gabriel conceived a long colonnade,

00:40:11.724 --> 00:40:14.125
made to look even longer by a play of mirrors.

00:40:15.186 --> 00:40:16.327
Thanks to its oval shape,

00:40:16.827 --> 00:40:19.308
every member of the audience had an unhindered view.

00:40:20.349 --> 00:40:21.650
The acoustics are perfect.

00:40:22.510 --> 00:40:23.631
Because it is made of wood,

00:40:24.131 --> 00:40:26.393
the house resonates like a rare violin.

00:40:27.633 --> 00:40:28.914
There are 1,000 seats.

00:40:29.694 --> 00:40:31.616
The orchestra pit seats 80 musicians,

00:40:32.016 --> 00:40:32.616
who play Lully,

00:40:33.257 --> 00:40:33.537
Rameau,

00:40:34.217 --> 00:40:34.517
Gluck.

00:40:36.659 --> 00:40:38.339
The king has his own box.

00:40:39.400 --> 00:40:41.882
He wants to see without being seen.

00:40:49.606 --> 00:40:49.846
Today,

00:40:49.906 --> 00:40:53.487
this opera house is the largest surviving court opera in Europe.

00:40:57.869 --> 00:41:00.290
Louis XV dies in 1774.

00:41:01.270 --> 00:41:04.031
A heavy past sits on the shoulders of the two young heirs,

00:41:04.491 --> 00:41:05.591
ill-prepared to reign.

00:41:06.732 --> 00:41:10.953
Painter Hubert Robert shows the royal couple watching the felling of trees in the park.

00:41:11.713 --> 00:41:12.754
They have grown too old.

00:41:14.114 --> 00:41:14.995
Prophetic canvas.

00:41:15.735 --> 00:41:16.335
The statues,

00:41:16.676 --> 00:41:18.377
a symbol of a glorious past,

00:41:18.717 --> 00:41:19.337
keep watch.

00:41:20.158 --> 00:41:21.639
The present is soft and light,

00:41:22.199 --> 00:41:27.723
but an uncertain future is mirrored in these grim skies and the desolation of these dying trees.

00:41:32.005 --> 00:41:33.186
Indecisive and shy,

00:41:33.806 --> 00:41:36.128
Louis XVI is more interested in science,

00:41:36.308 --> 00:41:36.748
the hunt,

00:41:37.068 --> 00:41:39.030
and find living than in governing.

00:41:40.058 --> 00:41:42.220
He likes to hide away in his comfortable rooms,

00:41:42.840 --> 00:41:44.321
especially his beloved library.

00:41:45.262 --> 00:41:47.724
Sculpted infants support the mantle of the fireplace.

00:41:48.644 --> 00:41:51.086
Across the front are gilded bas-reliefs.

00:41:56.730 --> 00:41:58.191
The king is a curious man.

00:41:58.992 --> 00:42:01.774
He is passionately interested in the voyages of discovery.

00:42:02.638 --> 00:42:08.502
He traces on his globe the route of Captain Cook's journey and works on mounting the expedition of his own navigator,

00:42:09.042 --> 00:42:09.823
La Perouse.

00:42:15.187 --> 00:42:20.670
Madame Vigée Lebrun captures the natural dignity of the Queen Marie-Antoinette in this painting.

00:42:21.651 --> 00:42:25.334
Marie-Antoinette detests Versailles and ridicules royal etiquette.

00:42:28.535 --> 00:42:29.496
Young and spirited,

00:42:29.896 --> 00:42:34.680
the French queen seeks diversion and pays no heed to the advice of wiser counselors.

00:42:35.641 --> 00:42:37.082
When she has to reside in the palace,

00:42:37.583 --> 00:42:42.026
she hides away with her favorites in her large apartment or her gilded salon.

00:42:49.275 --> 00:42:49.676
Reznia,

00:42:49.696 --> 00:42:52.862
the cabinetmaker who constructed the famous roll-top desk,

00:42:53.343 --> 00:42:59.014
makes her an ensemble of furniture exquisitely decorated in bronze and fabulously expensive.

00:43:19.151 --> 00:43:20.012
Not much of a reader,

00:43:20.512 --> 00:43:24.636
Marie Antoinette almost never visits the library designed by Richard Meek.

00:43:29.341 --> 00:43:29.801
Instead,

00:43:30.182 --> 00:43:32.844
she spends much of her time resting in her boudoir,

00:43:33.425 --> 00:43:34.106
the sofa room.

00:43:36.268 --> 00:43:40.492
Louis XVI redecorates this room for her after the birth of their first son.

00:43:41.373 --> 00:43:43.435
The Queen's favorite emblems are all there.

00:43:47.583 --> 00:43:48.544
The Habsburg eagle,

00:43:49.025 --> 00:43:51.808
which is even incorporated into the glass in the door.

00:43:53.711 --> 00:43:54.852
The rose for love.

00:43:56.093 --> 00:43:56.674
The peacock,

00:43:57.195 --> 00:43:58.116
the symbol of Juno,

00:43:58.436 --> 00:43:59.518
queen of the gods.

00:44:31.403 --> 00:44:34.564
The queen's bedroom is the epitome of dynastic glory.

00:44:36.344 --> 00:44:38.985
All the heirs to the throne are born here.

00:44:41.626 --> 00:44:43.446
Three queens have slept in this bed.

00:44:44.747 --> 00:44:47.267
The decoration is pure Marie Antoinette.

00:44:48.668 --> 00:44:49.628
Here she is in her room,

00:44:49.948 --> 00:44:51.609
surrounded by her ladies-in-waiting.

00:44:55.890 --> 00:44:58.310
The structure of the room goes back to Marie Thérèse,

00:44:58.751 --> 00:44:59.871
Louis XIV's wife.

00:45:00.903 --> 00:45:04.586
The woodwork and the ceiling decorations were made for Louis XV's wife,

00:45:05.007 --> 00:45:05.947
Marie Legzinska.

00:45:10.692 --> 00:45:13.714
The queens had to observe the same rituals for their toilette,

00:45:13.974 --> 00:45:16.196
their meals and their sleep as their husbands.

00:45:17.197 --> 00:45:19.079
Even childbirth was a public affair.

00:45:20.460 --> 00:45:22.882
Marie Antoinette reacts violently to this practice.

00:45:24.071 --> 00:45:27.372
Her first lady-in-waiting describes how in the final moments of labor,

00:45:27.853 --> 00:45:28.233
Vermont,

00:45:28.573 --> 00:45:29.373
the obstetrician,

00:45:29.814 --> 00:45:30.114
shouts,

00:45:30.314 --> 00:45:31.374
The queen is delivering

00:45:32.055 --> 00:45:34.055
The oglers thrust their way into the room.

00:45:35.036 --> 00:45:36.096
There were so many of them,

00:45:36.416 --> 00:45:37.317
and they were so loud,

00:45:37.717 --> 00:45:39.598
that I thought the queen would die of fear.

00:45:45.140 --> 00:45:46.701
Untouched since Louis XIV,

00:45:47.281 --> 00:45:50.623
the ceiling in the salon of the nobles is dedicated to Mercury.

00:45:51.695 --> 00:45:53.236
Here the Queen holds audience.

00:46:02.960 --> 00:46:05.021
This antechamber is reserved for the Great Meal,

00:46:05.362 --> 00:46:06.942
which now only takes place on Sunday.

00:46:07.823 --> 00:46:09.544
Marie Antoinette just makes an appearance.

00:46:09.864 --> 00:46:11.284
She doesn't even take her gloves off.

00:46:12.145 --> 00:46:13.546
Why bother coming to Versailles,

00:46:13.547 --> 00:46:14.046
people wonder.

00:46:14.426 --> 00:46:16.427
We never see the King or the Queen anymore.

00:46:17.159 --> 00:46:19.620
In response to the increasingly virulent attacks on her,

00:46:20.020 --> 00:46:21.741
Marie Antoinette commissions this portrait.

00:46:22.201 --> 00:46:23.642
She is depicted in her majesty,

00:46:24.142 --> 00:46:25.582
mother to the heiress to the throne,

00:46:25.662 --> 00:46:26.883
sovereign queen of France.

00:46:27.363 --> 00:46:28.804
But it is already too late.

00:46:29.484 --> 00:46:31.225
This picture is dated 1787,

00:46:31.505 --> 00:46:33.426
only two years before the revolution.

00:46:35.446 --> 00:46:36.567
In this climate of discord,

00:46:36.987 --> 00:46:39.008
the Trianon plays an ill-fated role.

00:46:39.748 --> 00:46:40.668
But let's back up a bit.

00:46:42.349 --> 00:46:44.190
At the encouragement of Madame de Pompadour,

00:46:44.770 --> 00:46:46.191
the Trianon had been expanded.

00:46:47.047 --> 00:46:51.309
The Marquise was the only person who could entertain and distract the difficult Louis XV.

00:46:52.569 --> 00:46:54.890
She managed to get him interested in the Trianon project,

00:46:55.190 --> 00:46:57.271
which she had worked on with the architect Gabriel.

00:47:00.413 --> 00:47:02.253
In the new pavilion with its French garden,

00:47:02.734 --> 00:47:04.694
Louis XV could keep his herbariums.

00:47:05.935 --> 00:47:08.056
There were concerts and dainty little meals.

00:47:08.976 --> 00:47:10.157
The frieze of roosters,

00:47:10.417 --> 00:47:13.578
chickens and turkeys mirrors the nearby menagerie,

00:47:14.118 --> 00:47:15.339
full of barnyard animals.

00:47:18.595 --> 00:47:20.356
Fifteen years after the French pavilion,

00:47:20.816 --> 00:47:23.938
Gabriel built the little Trianon castle to modern taste,

00:47:24.778 --> 00:47:25.859
a Grecian design.

00:47:27.260 --> 00:47:28.120
Madame de Pompadour,

00:47:28.200 --> 00:47:29.941
who died in 1764,

00:47:30.502 --> 00:47:32.223
never lived to see it completed.

00:47:42.288 --> 00:47:44.049
There is a break here with the Rococo,

00:47:44.690 --> 00:47:47.793
a movement towards classicism that can be seen in this stairwell.

00:47:49.214 --> 00:47:54.158
Its simplicity is a stark contrast to the ornate banister festoon with Marie Antoinette's monogram,

00:47:54.778 --> 00:47:56.079
a reminder that as a gift,

00:47:56.580 --> 00:47:59.202
Louis XVI has given her the Trianon.

00:48:07.309 --> 00:48:10.811
The audience room is the largest of all the rooms in the Queen's quarters.

00:48:17.496 --> 00:48:18.516
Flowers abound,

00:48:19.757 --> 00:48:20.917
lilies and roses,

00:48:23.278 --> 00:48:24.018
sunflowers,

00:48:25.378 --> 00:48:29.140
even the flowers that figure in the myths of Narcissus or Hyacinth.

00:48:30.740 --> 00:48:32.441
The Queen is alone here for long,

00:48:32.941 --> 00:48:34.001
too long holidays.

00:48:35.001 --> 00:48:39.943
The Lady of the Manor admits only members of the royal family and a few chosen friends in her presence.

00:48:40.803 --> 00:48:41.443
Protocol is,

00:48:41.444 --> 00:48:41.904
of course,

00:48:42.064 --> 00:48:42.804
dispensed with.

00:49:12.307 --> 00:49:16.908
The rooms open out onto lawns that have been lined with Louis XV's pride and joy.

00:49:17.568 --> 00:49:19.829
Long greenhouses designed by Jussieu.

00:49:20.849 --> 00:49:22.370
Marie-Antoinette had them raised.

00:49:25.071 --> 00:49:29.172
Marie-Antoinette's love of nature repeats itself in her extraordinary bedroom chairs.

00:49:29.936 --> 00:49:31.137
They were ordered from Jacob,

00:49:31.497 --> 00:49:33.219
the most daring carpenter of the day.

00:49:43.326 --> 00:49:44.087
Pine cones,

00:49:44.787 --> 00:49:47.049
wheat stalks mingle with lily of the valley.

00:49:47.690 --> 00:49:49.951
Precious fabrics are embroidered with wool.

00:49:54.436 --> 00:49:54.897
Nearby,

00:49:55.398 --> 00:49:56.320
the Temple of Love,

00:49:56.380 --> 00:49:57.943
with its brooks and rustic air,

00:49:58.384 --> 00:50:00.648
sits in the newly constructed English Garden.

00:50:02.071 --> 00:50:05.598
This little cockeyed room was created especially for Marie Antoinette.

00:50:56.628 --> 00:51:01.789
The sliding mirrors seem to express the Queen's desire to hide away from the world.

00:51:11.492 --> 00:51:12.232
Another escape,

00:51:12.932 --> 00:51:13.433
the theater.

00:51:18.354 --> 00:51:19.074
On this stage,

00:51:19.394 --> 00:51:21.755
Marie-Antoinette acts out her favorite roles.

00:51:22.635 --> 00:51:24.376
She plays Rosine from Beaumarchais'

00:51:24.736 --> 00:51:25.476
Marriage of Figaro.

00:51:26.160 --> 00:51:28.381
which Louis XVI had banned in Paris.

00:51:38.285 --> 00:51:41.886
The house still has all its original machinery and all its rigging.

00:51:42.947 --> 00:51:43.907
Now all is silent,

00:51:44.467 --> 00:51:46.068
waiting for the curtain to rise again.

00:52:00.444 --> 00:52:04.666
This scenic decor from 1750 is among the oldest in the world.

00:52:22.676 --> 00:52:22.796
At

00:52:22.797 --> 00:52:24.457
the edge of the Trianon's domain,

00:52:25.078 --> 00:52:28.780
the architect Meek designs this make-believe shepherd's village for the queen.

00:52:29.820 --> 00:52:31.961
It has a dozen little rustic Norman houses,

00:52:32.662 --> 00:52:33.162
a farm,

00:52:33.663 --> 00:52:34.103
a dairy,

00:52:34.563 --> 00:52:35.144
a windmill,

00:52:35.624 --> 00:52:36.184
a pond,

00:52:36.705 --> 00:52:37.225
and of course,

00:52:37.505 --> 00:52:38.366
the Queen's house.

00:52:39.266 --> 00:52:40.607
It's a pastoral refuge.

00:52:46.631 --> 00:52:47.592
On October 5th,

00:52:48.132 --> 00:52:53.416
Marie Antoinette is at the Trianon when news comes that the mob is marching towards Versailles.

00:52:56.468 --> 00:52:57.068
The next day,

00:52:57.328 --> 00:53:00.410
the queen's apartment is invaded and her bodyguards slaughtered.

00:53:01.171 --> 00:53:02.471
The king gives in to the rebels.

00:53:03.132 --> 00:53:04.653
Forced departure for Paris.

00:53:07.634 --> 00:53:11.737
When the king and queen were guillotined in 1793 and 1794,

00:53:12.197 --> 00:53:13.457
at the height of the reign of terror,

00:53:14.158 --> 00:53:16.099
the palace is threatened with destruction.

00:53:17.260 --> 00:53:17.940
The people cry,

00:53:18.460 --> 00:53:19.461
let them plow it under.

00:53:20.301 --> 00:53:22.342
Potatoes are planted in the formal gardens.

00:53:22.943 --> 00:53:24.324
The furniture auctioned off.

00:53:25.184 --> 00:53:26.105
In the course of the year,

00:53:26.465 --> 00:53:29.727
17,000 lots fall under the auctioneer's hammer.

00:53:31.127 --> 00:53:34.750
The slow decline begins until the dawn comes again.

00:53:35.650 --> 00:53:38.172
For Versailles will outlive any revolution.

00:53:39.390 --> 00:53:40.491
and the fascination grows.

00:53:40.492 --> 00:53:44.032
In 1810,

00:53:44.072 --> 00:53:45.073
Emperor Napoleon I,

00:53:45.553 --> 00:53:46.994
riding here in front of the Trianon,

00:53:47.414 --> 00:53:50.015
is nourishing a desire to set up his court at Versailles.

00:53:50.735 --> 00:53:52.156
He has just married Marie-Louise,

00:53:52.636 --> 00:53:54.237
the great-niece of Marie-Antoinette.

00:53:54.857 --> 00:53:55.297
All the same,

00:53:55.337 --> 00:53:56.518
Versailles intimidates him.

00:53:57.558 --> 00:53:59.439
He prefers the Trianon and moves in.

00:54:00.220 --> 00:54:01.800
The emperor's furniture is still there,

00:54:02.060 --> 00:54:04.782
so much so that he feels more present than his predecessors.

00:54:06.358 --> 00:54:06.918
In his room,

00:54:07.519 --> 00:54:10.541
Louis XV's paneling disappears behind the bed,

00:54:11.261 --> 00:54:14.284
and the chairs are recovered in cream-colored silk,

00:54:14.724 --> 00:54:15.805
embroidered with silver.

00:54:16.825 --> 00:54:18.527
But time and tide wait for no man.

00:54:19.127 --> 00:54:19.868
Five years later,

00:54:19.888 --> 00:54:21.068
with the defeat at Waterloo,

00:54:21.489 --> 00:54:22.649
the empire falls.

00:54:25.031 --> 00:54:26.752
Crowned king in 1830,

00:54:26.753 --> 00:54:29.214
Louis-Philippe does his best to save Versailles,

00:54:29.734 --> 00:54:30.555
but to what purpose?

00:54:31.376 --> 00:54:34.218
He decides to make Versailles the symbol of the nation.

00:54:36.858 --> 00:54:37.778
1837,

00:54:38.299 --> 00:54:39.699
the palace becomes a museum,

00:54:40.039 --> 00:54:42.280
a shrine to the bygone glories of France.

00:54:43.141 --> 00:54:47.802
Louis-Philippe wants to unite the French people who have been torn apart by rival factions.

00:54:51.664 --> 00:54:56.106
He tries to appease the nobility by consecrating five rooms to the Crusades.

00:55:00.334 --> 00:55:07.418
The dominant symbol of the main room is a 16th century cedar door that originally stood in Rhodes at the hospice of the Knights of St.

00:55:07.458 --> 00:55:08.318
John of Jerusalem.

00:55:09.319 --> 00:55:14.421
It was a curious neo-gothic touch in the Baroque and Rococo halls of Versailles.

00:55:15.482 --> 00:55:18.343
Louis-Philippe chose the most renowned painters for the canvases.

00:55:19.424 --> 00:55:20.444
Delacroix creates

00:55:20.785 --> 00:55:23.006
Entry of the Crusaders into Constantinople.

00:55:26.018 --> 00:55:30.961
The former annex of the princes on the first floor becomes a 120 meter long battle gallery.

00:55:31.882 --> 00:55:37.025
35 canvases and 75 busts are a monumental summary of France's military history,

00:55:37.685 --> 00:55:38.726
at least its victories.

00:55:40.907 --> 00:55:41.347
Clovis,

00:55:41.407 --> 00:55:42.448
the founder of France,

00:55:42.768 --> 00:55:45.009
victorious at Tolbiac in 496.

00:55:48.011 --> 00:55:51.953
Saint-Louis fighting at Taillebourg in 1242 by Delacroix.

00:55:56.254 --> 00:56:00.295
François I at Marignan in 1515 by Fragonard the son.

00:56:04.456 --> 00:56:09.758
Louis XV and Marshal de Saxe at Fontenoy in 1745 by Horace Vernet.

00:56:12.719 --> 00:56:17.040
Napoleon at Austerlitz in 1805 by the Baron François Gérald.

00:56:20.401 --> 00:56:24.982
It is thanks to Louis-Philippe that Napoleon's presence is so dominant at Versailles.

00:56:25.666 --> 00:56:28.489
There are many nostalgic tributes to the Napoleonic era.

00:56:30.531 --> 00:56:34.254
David's famous Coronation of Napoleon gives its name to this gallery,

00:56:34.795 --> 00:56:35.856
the Coronation Gallery.

00:56:38.558 --> 00:56:43.904
It has been redecorated in high empire style and houses another of David's canvases,

00:56:44.584 --> 00:56:45.765
Distribution of Eagles.

00:56:53.378 --> 00:56:58.442
The length of the suite of rooms on the ground floor of the South Wing also honors the Napoleonic era.

00:57:02.626 --> 00:57:06.990
Giraudet's revolt in Cairo dramatizes this event in the Egyptian campaign.

00:57:17.539 --> 00:57:20.142
Napoleon also appears high up on the top floor.

00:57:21.066 --> 00:57:22.908
Painted by Baron Gault,

00:57:22.909 --> 00:57:28.035
Napoleon in Italy rushes forward under a barrage of bullets to take the bridge of Arcola.

00:57:34.403 --> 00:57:39.309
Twenty rooms illustrate the twenty years of Napoleonic influence over the destiny of France.

00:57:47.490 --> 00:57:47.750
Thus,

00:57:48.231 --> 00:57:51.033
Versailles is the major museum dedicated to Napoleon,

00:57:51.494 --> 00:57:55.837
a museum that records the history of France from the Middle Ages to the 19th century.

00:57:58.660 --> 00:58:00.521
The palace lives on today.

00:58:03.448 --> 00:58:03.668
In its

00:58:04.188 --> 00:58:05.009
800-hectare estate,

00:58:05.509 --> 00:58:11.971
millions of tourists can visit 120 historical galleries and 120 rooms of the royal residence.

00:58:13.951 --> 00:58:16.532
The treasures dispersed by the revolution have come home.

00:58:17.472 --> 00:58:19.113
In Louis XVI's games room,

00:58:19.473 --> 00:58:21.934
the proper furniture has found its proper place.

00:58:26.515 --> 00:58:29.736
Marie Antoinette's dinner service awaits the Queen's Supper.

00:58:32.656 --> 00:58:35.199
The vast dining room is ready for a formal banquet.

00:58:35.979 --> 00:58:38.422
Louis XV decor has been reinstalled.

00:58:41.405 --> 00:58:45.609
The new carriage museum in the Royal Stables displays gala coaches.

00:58:46.310 --> 00:58:47.371
Royal carriages.

00:59:03.096 --> 00:59:04.017
And in the gardens,

00:59:04.617 --> 00:59:07.940
Enceladus Grove has been restored to its former splendor.

00:59:18.549 --> 00:59:19.590
Throughout the estate,

00:59:20.110 --> 00:59:22.252
the original hydraulic system functions,

00:59:22.913 --> 00:59:24.534
giving life to this fairyland.

00:59:37.776 --> 00:59:40.399
There are 200,000 trees to prune and care for,

00:59:41.120 --> 00:59:43.582
210,000 flowers to be planted every year,

00:59:44.203 --> 00:59:45.945
as well as more than 2,000 windows,

00:59:46.265 --> 00:59:47.146
700 rooms,

00:59:47.287 --> 00:59:48.568
67 staircases,

00:59:48.968 --> 00:59:50.911
11,000 square meters of roof.

00:59:52.292 --> 00:59:53.433
It's splendor intact.

00:59:54.014 --> 00:59:54.635
Versailles,

00:59:54.636 --> 00:59:56.657
